A||||14||Dustin||mechanics and fieros
Z||000000||Dustin||05-26-99||10:44 PM||||im looking at a fiero or two around town here  (nebraska), and i mentioned a fiero to my mom, and she immediatly said "You wouldnt like those.  They have alot of mechanical problems."  how true is that??  what problems at fieros known to have??  thanks<p>Dustin
Z||000001||ChadMan||05-27-99||07:15 AM||||I only know of one chronic problem with Fieros, and that's the clutch.  Stock Fiero clutches don't stand up to much abuse and the hydraulic master/slave cylinders are often problematic too.  Some early Fieros (especially '84s) suffer from electrical problems...not expensive, but irritating.  Check the coolant, too...if it's rusty, stay away!<p>Other than that, my experience has been that Fieros are very reliable.  All the engines and transmissions available in Fieros are pretty durable and will stand up to hard use if you maintain them properly.  Fiero bodies never rust (of course) and the frames are very rust-resistant too, unless they've been bent and straightened.  Many mechanical parts are shared with other GM cars, so maintenance is cheap.<p>If you buy a Fiero, you'll probably have to invest $500 or so to make it right, after which it will give you years of faithful service.  Good luck, Dustin!  
Z||000002||Hock||05-27-99||12:01 PM||||in adition to that you also have to be careful of the rear calipers. when it comes time to change the pads you have to use a special tool to retract the pistons. also if you don't use the e-brake reguarly you might have to replace them (if the tool don't work). so go find youself one and enjoy it.
