Z||000006||Pretender||05-27-99||03:56 PM||||a C-CLAMP AND A PIECE OF WOOD won't back in the rear piston in the caliper!, it must be turned(left i think)...i bought one of those special tools from napa but when we did the brakes for my friends crx we were able to use a pair of needle nose pliers, i never thought about using them., it might of saved me like 8 dollars or something.
Z||000007||PaUL||05-27-99||04:45 PM||||yeah needle nose pliers, thats what I use. I never knew there was a special tool for this. Just stick the tips of the pliers into the 2 holes in the piston and turn.
Z||000008||theogre||05-27-99||06:45 PM||||turning the piston in Fiero's rear caliper is a realy good way to scrag them completely.<p>the proper method for this caliper is:<p>1. remove the e-brake arm.<br>2. place a large socket over the e-brake actuator. (17mm or larger, it must completely clear the bolt and seal.)<br>3. press the piston in with a c-clamp.<p>As you press it the e-brake bolt will spin. DO NOT press on the e-brake bolt! I've used this method and it works exactly as described in the Haynes book. This caliper is Not designed for the turn the piston method. You will likely damage the caliper if you turn the piston.<p>This also gives you a chance to check the e-brake "bolt" outer seal, and bearing washer. If either show signs of damage they must be replaced. ((The thrust washer is suposed to be replaced every time the arm is removed, but if it's in good shape I reuse it.)) If any leakage is showing the caliper must be rebuilt.
